;; talk/trader--protocol.scm — objections to surfaces/trader.scm, lens: protocol.

(objection 'o-trader-protocol-1 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'trader-default-sign-inside-limits :lens "protocol"
  :claim (canon 'swap-cancel-ask-only
    "This default signs a resting order at once, and d-trader-ask-before-sign lists what the trader is asked about (raising a credit line, moving money off a hub, leaving, a settlement, a price far from best) without including placing an order that rests. A resting order cannot be taken back by the trader: cancellation is only a request, the hub decides, and the amount stays held until it does (core/account/tx/handlers/swap/lifecycle/cancel.ts changes nothing in the account). The hub can therefore fill the order at the old price after the market has turned, and the limits the trader set do not bound that loss, because they cap how far a hub may owe the trader, not how far the price moves after the order is signed. The two-percent distance default is measured once, at placement. The home screen and the orders screen then offer a pull action under the job 'pull all my orders the moment the market turns', which the protocol cannot do against a hub that is slow or silent. The cost is a trader who believes a signed-at-once order is one they can withdraw, and who learns otherwise when it fills."))

(objection 'o-trader-protocol-2 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'trader-ticket :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/swap/resolve/settlement.ts"
    "The ticket answers 'what can I trade here right now' and shows can-send and credit-left-to-them, but never can-receive. The fill check in settlement.ts compares the amount the trader wants with the hub's own outCapacity in the token the trader receives, and refuses the fill when the hub's room is smaller. So the number that decides whether an order can complete is on the hub's side and is not on the screen. A trader sizes an order from can-send, places it, and it rests without being able to fill, with the offered amount held and cancellation only a request. The decision d-trader-hub-limits cites this very check as its reason, then the ticket leaves out the figure that shows it. The trader is also told nothing about how old the room figure is, though it moves with every other fill at that hub."))

(objection 'o-trader-protocol-3 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'trader-leaving :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/dispute/finalize.ts"
    "The screen asks 'when do I get my money back' and shows one window, exit-ends-at. That time applies to the secured share only. Whatever the hub owed beyond the collateral is paid from the hub's money on the chain if it has any, and otherwise stays a debt that earns nothing and can be collected only from later arrivals, so it can be lost entirely (law unsecured-no-seizure). The screen puts owed-to-me-unsecured next to a date and lets a date stand for it. The window is also not always the one shown: whoever starts the dispute waits the whole window, and when a cross-chain swap leg is open on the account the hub cannot end it early and both sides wait for every swap deadline as well (law exit-early-if-answered). The trader surface carries cross-chain swaps, and this screen shows no open leg and no swap deadline, so the date shown can be earlier than the real one. The twenty-five hours in the decision is also the code as it stands today, and the source document says that rule may change."))

(objection 'o-trader-protocol-4 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'trader-limits-flow :lens "protocol"
  :claim (canon 'credit-cut-keeps-debt
    "The flow ends with the trader knowing 'the most a hub can come to owe me', and the job is to cap exposure. Lowering the credit line only blocks new debt and cannot erase what the hub already owes; so after a cut the trader's exposure at that hub can stay above the new line, and nothing in the flow shows the difference between the new line and the amount still owed. The second tool the flow offers, automatic collateral top-up, is a request, not a guarantee. The fee is taken from the trader's side at the moment of asking and comes back only if the hub sends it back (core/account/tx/handlers/rebalance/request-collateral.ts); only one request per token can be open, and while it is open no further request is made, so a hub that is slow can leave unsecured debt growing past the trigger with no second request. The default turns this on and signs it without a question. The flow's wording 'when their wallet will ask for more collateral and the most it will pay' is accurate, but the screen frames it as a cap on loss, and the protocol gives the trader only a cap on new debt and a prepaid request."))

(objection 'o-trader-protocol-5 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'trader-move-flow :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/htlc/resolve.ts"
    "The flow ends with the trader knowing the money 'arrived at the second hub'. What arrives there is secured only up to the second hub's collateral share; receiving beyond it is trusting the hub, bounded by the credit line the trader granted it (figure can-receive, which this flow and the home screen never show). With the default of zero credit the second hub can receive only what it has locked, so a move sized from what the trader has can be refused for lack of receiving room, which the trader cannot see before trying. Once the payment is on its way the trader cannot recall it before its deadline (law payer-cannot-recall), and the deadline is about a day in async mode, so 'in minutes' holds only in the instant mode. The decision already notes that paying oneself through the hubs is unconfirmed; the flow nonetheless lists it as a signed path and its ending does not say whether the arrived money is secured or owed."))

(objection 'o-trader-protocol-6 :by "sonnet-protocol" :to 'trader-cross-flow :lens "protocol"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/cross-j/salvage.ts"
    "The job is to buy on one chain and sell on another 'without being left holding one side', and the flow's ending promises recovery 'under their own key'. The recovery path in the code is a port of the proof the hub publishes when it claims the source leg; the code comments say the source-chain registration is the only recovery trigger. If the hub never claims, there is no proof to port, and if the trader's wallet is not running before the target deadline the port is not made. The surface shows only the amount and deadline of a leg (and the ground itself marks the cross-leg amount meaning as a guess), so the trader cannot see which of those cases they are in, or that recovery needs their entity to act before a deadline. The protocol can end with one leg settled and the other waiting on the hub, which is the state the job says it prevents."))
